321 | 4 | {"request_id": "chatcmpl-8f820dfcc13c5c5a", "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 49, "total_tokens": 561, "completion_tokens": 512, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 0, "created_cache_tokens": 0}, "completion_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 0}}, "finish_reason": "length", "ttft_s": 0.36247244700007286, "decode_s": 18.87869550099913, "client_decode_tok_s": 27.06754817741279, "e2e_s": 19.24127941000006, "text_sha256": "537da6f2de62185f2d8bd9bbd6611ec97d468800bdfe0282a3d731d516895023", "text": "# Practical Guide: Investigating a Slow Distributed Inference Service\n\nDistributed inference systems (e.g., LLM serving, large-scale vision models) are complex stacks involving networking, GPU/TPU hardware, CPU orchestration, and memory management. A \"slow\" service is rarely caused by a single factor; it is usually a symptom of a bottleneck in one of four layers: **Compute**, **Memory**, **Communication**, or **Orchestration**.\n\nThis guide provides a systematic approach to isolating these bottlenecks using concrete experiments and tools.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 1: Establish a Reliable Baseline & Avoid Misleading Benchmarks\n\nBefore debugging, you must ensure your measurements are valid. Many \"slow\" services are actually fast but misconfigured or mis-measured.\n\n### Common Pitfalls & How to Avoid Them\n\n| Pitfall | Why It\u2019s Misleading | Mitigation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Cold Start Bias** | First requests include model loading, JIT compilation, and cache warming. | **Warm up** the service with 10\u201350 dummy requests before measuring. |\n| **Client-Side Bottleneck** | The client (Python script, browser) may be slower than the server. | Use a high-performance load generator (e.g., `wrk`, `hey`, `locust`, or `k6`). Monitor client CPU usage. |\n| **Latency vs. Throughput Confusion** | Low latency at low concurrency \u2260 high throughput at high concurrency. | Measure **P50, P95, P99 latency** AND **requests per second (RPS)** at increasing concurrency levels. |\n| **Batching Artifacts** | Dynamic batching can hide per-request latency. | Measure both **single-request latency** (batch size=1) and **throughput** (batch size=N). |\n| **Network Noise** | Shared cloud networks have variable jitter. | Run tests in the same availability zone/VPC. Use `ping` and `iperf3` to baseline network health. |\n\n### Step 1: Define Your SLA\n- **Latency-Sensitive**: e.g., Chatbot (P99 < 500ms).\n- **Throughput-Sensitive**: e.g., Batch scoring (Max RPS).\n- **Cost-Sensitive**: e.g., Maximize tokens/sec per GPU.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 2: Systematic Bott", "length": 512, "repeat": 2, "warmup": false, "request": {"model": "qwen38-w4-pipeline-c1-dispatch", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Write a practical guide to investigating a slow distributed inference service. Include concrete experiments, how to isolate CPU, memory, communication and accelerator bottlenecks, and how to avoid misleading benchmarks."}], "max_tokens": 512, "temperature": 0, "seed": 42, "ignore_eos": true, "chat_template_kwargs": {"enable_thinking": false}, "stream": true, "stream_options": {"include_usage": true}}, "drafted_delta": 426.0, "accepted_delta": 298.0, "acceptance": 0.6995305164319249}
